---
metadata:
  title: "About Annie Elliot | Author of Mr & Mrs Charles Dickens"
  description: "Annie Elliot is the author of Mr & Mrs Charles Dickens, Her Story. She has an MA in Creative Writing and a BA (Hons) in Literary Studies."
  canonical: "https://annieelliot.co.uk/author/"
  pageType: "author"
---

<section id="author" class="u-container">
	<h1>About the Author</h1>
	<div>
		<div class="u-grid main-grid">
			<!--
				The ladder, declared rather than inherited. The portrait's widest
				layout is the single-column tier just under 60rem — the container's
				content box, ~894 CSS px — and above 60rem it is one half of the
				1160px content box less the 5rem grid gap, so 540 CSS px. 1200
				therefore covers 2x at the wide-viewport size and 1800 covers 2x of
				the widest the photo is ever laid out, which is also the cap
				src/reviews.webc declares, so the site has one number.
				Without a width here the image inherited the global ladder, and
				before "auto" came off that list it emitted a 2837px rendition —
				the source's full width — in both WebP and JPEG.
			-->
			<img
				webc:is="eleventy-image"
				src="assets/images/author.jpg"
				alt="Portrait of Annie Elliot"
				width="400, 640, 800, 1200, 1800"
				sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 50vw, 100vw"
			/>

			<div>
				<p>
					Annie Elliot has an MA in Creative Writing and a BA (Hons) in Literary
					Studies. Before retiring she worked in local government, as a
					newspaper reporter, and taught communication. She has written a
					booklet to help teachers understand dyslexic children’s difficulties.
					Her short stories and flash fiction have been successful in literary
					competitions and she has been longlisted for the Mslexia Novel Award.
					She writes and performs monologues.
				</p>

				<p>
					At university, her tutor, the late Fay Weldon, described Annie Elliot
					as a natural writer and her work as ‘completely brilliant’. Struck by
					her talent for the acerbic and moving, the author and screenwriter
					Nikesh Shukla, host of the Brown Baby podcast, complimented Annie on
					her ‘comedian’s eye for detail and absurdity’. The naturalist, author
					and broadcaster Stephen Moss described her bitter-sweet style as funny
					and tragic. Mr & Mrs Charles Dickens is Annie Elliot’s debut novel.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	#author {
		padding-block: var(--space-l);

		h1 {
			text-align: center;
			color: var(--taupe);
			margin: 0;
			font-family: var(--font-base);
			margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
		}

		.main-grid {
			gap: var(--space-2xl);
			text-align: justify;

			@media all and (min-width: 60rem) {
				grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
			}

			div > p:first-of-type {
				margin-top: 0;
			}
		}

		picture {
			display: block;

			img {
				width: 100%;
				display: block;
				/*
					The portrait is a 2:3 photograph, far taller than the prose beside
					it. Cropped from the bottom, which is where the empty shoulder room
					is, so the face survives whatever the frame becomes.
				*/
				aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
				height: auto;
				object-fit: cover;
				object-position: 50% 10%;
			}

			/*
				Two columns: the photo takes its height from the prose, not the other
				way round. A normally-flowed image is what sizes the grid row, so it
				is taken out of flow — that leaves <picture> with no intrinsic height,
				the row is measured from the text column alone, and the stretched
				<picture> hands that height straight back to the image.
			*/
			@media all and (min-width: 60rem) {
				position: relative;

				img {
					position: absolute;
					inset: 0;
					width: 100%;
					height: 100%;
					aspect-ratio: auto;
				}
			}
		}
	}
</style>
